  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A number NNN is said to be XXX-valid if NNN is even, and all numbers from {1,2,...,N}\\{1, 2, ..., N\\}{1,2,...,N} can be split into N2\\frac{N}{2}2N​ mutually exclusive pairs of 222, where each pair has a sum which is a power of XXX.\n\nGiven NNN and XXX, find the number of integers iii satisfying 1≤i≤N1 \\le i \\le N1≤i≤N and iii is XXX-valid.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of a single line of input.\n\nThe first line of input contains NNN and XXX - the parameters defined in the statement.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the number of XXX-valid numbers iii such that 1≤i≤N1 \\le i \\le N1≤i≤N.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1031 \\le T \\le 10^31≤T≤103",
      "1≤N≤10151 \\le N \\le 10^{15}1≤N≤1015",
      "1≤X≤1031 \\le X \\le 10^31≤X≤103"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n27 3\n4 2\n4 5",
        "output": "7\n0\n1",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest Case 1 : It can be proven that 222 and 666 are 333-valid numbers.\n\n\nConstruction for 222 : Divide {1,2}\\{1, 2\\}{1,2} into {(1,2)}\\{(1, 2)\\}{(1,2)}. Thus sum of the only pair in this list is 333, which is a trivial power of 333.\n\n\nConstruction for 666 : Divide {1,2,3,4,5,6}\\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\\}{1,2,3,4,5,6} into {(1,2),(3,6),(4,5)}\\{(1, 2), (3, 6), (4, 5)\\}{(1,2),(3,6),(4,5)}. The sums of the pairs are {3,9,9}\\{3, 9, 9\\}{3,9,9} all of which are powers of 333.\n\n\nIt can be proven that there are exactly 777 numbers ≤27\\le 27≤27 which are 333-valid.\nTest Case 3 : 44 4 is 555-valid while 111, 222 and 333 are not."
      }
    ]
  },
